WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.000 --> 00:00:02.250
MARTIN OMANDER: Hi, Guillaume.

00:00:02.250 --> 00:00:04.140
In a previous episode,
Mete showed us

00:00:04.140 --> 00:00:06.840
Pic-a-Daily, the cool
picture sharing application

00:00:06.840 --> 00:00:08.280
that you guys built together.

00:00:08.280 --> 00:00:09.210
GUILLAUME LAFORGE: Hi, Martin.

00:00:09.210 --> 00:00:09.720
Oh yeah.

00:00:09.720 --> 00:00:12.990
That was a lot of fun to build
it using all those Google Cloud

00:00:12.990 --> 00:00:14.755
service technologies.

00:00:14.755 --> 00:00:15.630
MARTIN OMANDER: Yeah.

00:00:15.630 --> 00:00:18.300
And Mete told me that you've
rebuilt the application

00:00:18.300 --> 00:00:19.110
recently?

00:00:19.110 --> 00:00:20.318
GUILLAUME LAFORGE: Well, yes.

00:00:20.318 --> 00:00:22.320
The application used
the choreography pattern

00:00:22.320 --> 00:00:24.540
with loosely coupled
microservices

00:00:24.540 --> 00:00:26.250
communicating with the events.

00:00:26.250 --> 00:00:28.990
But we felt it needed
a bit of a refresh.

00:00:28.990 --> 00:00:32.565
We decided to convert it to
the orchestration pattern.

00:00:32.565 --> 00:00:37.149
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:43.890 --> 00:00:45.540
MARTIN OMANDER:
Orchestration pattern?

00:00:45.540 --> 00:00:46.740
What do you mean?

00:00:46.740 --> 00:00:48.323
GUILLAUME LAFORGE:
The idea is to have

00:00:48.323 --> 00:00:51.420
somehow a central brain,
the orchestrator, that

00:00:51.420 --> 00:00:54.780
orchestrates and calls
all the services directly.

00:00:54.780 --> 00:00:57.510
One pain point with
the choreography

00:00:57.510 --> 00:01:01.380
of even driven apps is that
it's sometimes a bit complicated

00:01:01.380 --> 00:01:03.210
to figure out what's going on.

00:01:03.210 --> 00:01:07.920
An orchestrator gives high level
view of your business processes

00:01:07.920 --> 00:01:10.260
to track their execution.

00:01:10.260 --> 00:01:13.590
In the loosely coupled
scenario when something fails,

00:01:13.590 --> 00:01:15.870
you usually have to
hunt down through heaps

00:01:15.870 --> 00:01:18.930
of uncorrelated logs
and errors to see

00:01:18.930 --> 00:01:22.380
which particular
service failed and why.

00:01:22.380 --> 00:01:23.940
But with an
orchestrator, you can

00:01:23.940 --> 00:01:26.760
see the execution of your
workflow in the cloud console

00:01:26.760 --> 00:01:30.678
and see the result of all the
service calls at a glance.

00:01:30.678 --> 00:01:31.470
MARTIN OMANDER: Oh.

00:01:31.470 --> 00:01:33.270
So what did you change
to help with that?

00:01:33.270 --> 00:01:35.293
And tell me more
about orchestration.

00:01:35.293 --> 00:01:36.960
GUILLAUME LAFORGE:
Google Cloud recently

00:01:36.960 --> 00:01:39.300
released a new product
called Workflows.

00:01:39.300 --> 00:01:40.710
It's an orchestrator.

00:01:40.710 --> 00:01:42.690
It allows developers
to create workflows.

00:01:42.690 --> 00:01:46.620
With a series of steps, you
can create business processes

00:01:46.620 --> 00:01:49.440
that orchestrate calls
to several services.

00:01:49.440 --> 00:01:54.180
And the workflow calls those
services as simple web APIs.

00:01:54.180 --> 00:01:57.000
So we gave Workflows
a try to get a clearer

00:01:57.000 --> 00:02:00.000
vision and understanding
of the underlying business

00:02:00.000 --> 00:02:02.370
processes of our app.

00:02:02.370 --> 00:02:04.120
MARTIN OMANDER: Wow,
that sounds exciting.

00:02:04.120 --> 00:02:06.130
Can you tell us more about
this new architecture

00:02:06.130 --> 00:02:08.562
and how Workflows fit
in the big picture?

00:02:08.562 --> 00:02:10.270
GUILLAUME LAFORGE:
Our app business logic

00:02:10.270 --> 00:02:11.440
isn't very complicated.

00:02:11.440 --> 00:02:16.120
But it can be drawn as a
simple flowchart basically.

00:02:16.120 --> 00:02:18.580
MARTIN OMANDER: OK, it looks
like the Google Cloud Console

00:02:18.580 --> 00:02:19.300
here.

00:02:19.300 --> 00:02:23.530
The Workflow's UI shows both a
YAML definition file as well as

00:02:23.530 --> 00:02:25.212
the flowchart diagram
of that workflow.

00:02:25.212 --> 00:02:26.170
GUILLAUME LAFORGE: Yep.

00:02:26.170 --> 00:02:26.770
Exactly.

00:02:26.770 --> 00:02:29.300
You write the YAML
definition on the left,

00:02:29.300 --> 00:02:32.590
but you can immediately
see the equivalent diagram

00:02:32.590 --> 00:02:34.115
on the right.

00:02:34.115 --> 00:02:34.990
MARTIN OMANDER: Cool.

00:02:34.990 --> 00:02:36.790
Could you describe this
particular workflow

00:02:36.790 --> 00:02:37.980
a little bit?

00:02:37.980 --> 00:02:38.980
GUILLAUME LAFORGE: Sure.

00:02:38.980 --> 00:02:41.950
When a new picture is
uploaded in Cloud Storage,

00:02:41.950 --> 00:02:44.710
or an existing one
is deleted, an event

00:02:44.710 --> 00:02:49.490
is fired, one for file
creation, one for file deletion.

00:02:49.490 --> 00:02:51.430
Those events trigger
two functions

00:02:51.430 --> 00:02:52.570
for those two situations.

00:02:52.570 --> 00:02:55.798
But both start our
workflow execution.

00:02:55.798 --> 00:02:56.590
MARTIN OMANDER: OK.

00:02:56.590 --> 00:02:57.610
That's how it starts.

00:02:57.610 --> 00:02:58.540
What happens next?

00:02:58.540 --> 00:03:00.790
GUILLAUME LAFORGE: If the
file event isn't recognized,

00:03:00.790 --> 00:03:03.040
we'll go straight to
the end of the workflow.

00:03:03.040 --> 00:03:05.380
If the file event
is a deletion, we

00:03:05.380 --> 00:03:07.720
remove the thumbnail
pictures from Cloud Storage

00:03:07.720 --> 00:03:10.210
as well as the picture
metadata in Firestore.

00:03:10.210 --> 00:03:12.220
If it's a file
creation event, we

00:03:12.220 --> 00:03:15.820
analyze the picture with machine
learning from the Vision API

00:03:15.820 --> 00:03:20.060
and extract information
about the labels, the color,

00:03:20.060 --> 00:03:22.510
whether it's safe or
not to show the picture.

00:03:22.510 --> 00:03:25.390
If it's a picture
safe to show publicly,

00:03:25.390 --> 00:03:27.580
we store the metadata
in Firestore,

00:03:27.580 --> 00:03:30.690
we create the thumbnail,
and the collage.

00:03:30.690 --> 00:03:31.880
MARTIN OMANDER: Ah, I see.

00:03:31.880 --> 00:03:35.352
So you're saying that it's the
workflow that calls those APIs?

00:03:35.352 --> 00:03:36.310
GUILLAUME LAFORGE: Yes.

00:03:36.310 --> 00:03:39.040
Calling an API is really
just a call declaration

00:03:39.040 --> 00:03:40.750
in the YAML definition.

00:03:40.750 --> 00:03:43.180
Notice for example
at the bottom how

00:03:43.180 --> 00:03:46.630
we select the features of the
Vision API that we want to use.

00:03:46.630 --> 00:03:49.480
Let me also mention the
new connectors feature

00:03:49.480 --> 00:03:51.760
in public preview for
now, which further

00:03:51.760 --> 00:03:56.620
simplifies calling some GCP APIs
with a dedicated call syntax.

00:03:56.620 --> 00:03:57.520
MARTIN OMANDER: Nice.

00:03:57.520 --> 00:03:59.680
Very declarative indeed.

00:03:59.680 --> 00:04:01.402
But there's still a
bit of code, right?

00:04:01.402 --> 00:04:02.610
GUILLAUME LAFORGE: Of course.

00:04:02.610 --> 00:04:04.810
For example, our
initial Cloud Function

00:04:04.810 --> 00:04:06.970
was calling APIs
programmatically.

00:04:06.970 --> 00:04:09.910
But there was a bit of logic
and data transformation

00:04:09.910 --> 00:04:11.030
code in the middle.

00:04:11.030 --> 00:04:14.680
So this logic part stayed but
the rest is all declarative

00:04:14.680 --> 00:04:15.340
now.

00:04:15.340 --> 00:04:17.890
MARTIN OMANDER: Wow, that's
less code to worry about.

00:04:17.890 --> 00:04:19.589
And also less event
handling maybe?

00:04:19.589 --> 00:04:20.589
GUILLAUME LAFORGE: True.

00:04:20.589 --> 00:04:21.730
Less event handling.

00:04:21.730 --> 00:04:23.900
Before with a
choreography approach,

00:04:23.900 --> 00:04:27.880
we had three different ways to
handle events, direct events

00:04:27.880 --> 00:04:31.210
from Cloud Functions,
Pub/Sub wrapped GCS even

00:04:31.210 --> 00:04:33.670
from Cloud Run, and
also Cloud Events

00:04:33.670 --> 00:04:36.370
payloads with even
arc to Cloud Run.

00:04:36.370 --> 00:04:39.160
Now it's raised API calls.

00:04:39.160 --> 00:04:40.840
MARTIN OMANDER:
That sounds awesome.

00:04:40.840 --> 00:04:42.642
So what did you learn
from this experience?

00:04:42.642 --> 00:04:44.725
GUILLAUME LAFORGE: It was
super refreshing to call

00:04:44.725 --> 00:04:47.860
the microservices via REST
instead of different event

00:04:47.860 --> 00:04:49.190
handling approaches.

00:04:49.190 --> 00:04:51.907
Also, did you see the
Workflows console?

00:04:51.907 --> 00:04:53.490
MARTIN OMANDER: Yeah,
that was the one

00:04:53.490 --> 00:04:56.970
with a flowchart and the YAML
definition side by side, right?

00:04:56.970 --> 00:04:59.370
That seems super handy for
understanding the process

00:04:59.370 --> 00:05:00.060
visually.

00:05:00.060 --> 00:05:01.050
GUILLAUME LAFORGE:
You'll get a bird's eye

00:05:01.050 --> 00:05:02.160
view of what's going on.

00:05:02.160 --> 00:05:05.670
And you can gain confidence in
making changes in your workflow

00:05:05.670 --> 00:05:09.720
without the fear of breaking
a large distributed system.

00:05:09.720 --> 00:05:13.200
And here on this screen, seeing
all the workflow execution

00:05:13.200 --> 00:05:14.310
is nice too.

00:05:14.310 --> 00:05:16.470
You know at a glance
for each execution

00:05:16.470 --> 00:05:18.690
if it succeeded or failed.

00:05:18.690 --> 00:05:21.630
I guess it's much easier to
debug when things go wrong.

00:05:21.630 --> 00:05:24.660
Also, we were able to
cut down some code,

00:05:24.660 --> 00:05:26.640
even remove some
services completely

00:05:26.640 --> 00:05:29.790
as they could be called
decoratively from the workflow.

00:05:29.790 --> 00:05:31.740
Same thing for error handling.

00:05:31.740 --> 00:05:34.410
That can be done
from the Workflow.

00:05:34.410 --> 00:05:35.250
MARTIN OMANDER: OK.

00:05:35.250 --> 00:05:37.708
Was it easier to set
up and configure?

00:05:37.708 --> 00:05:39.750
GUILLAUME LAFORGE: Yes,
that was another benefit.

00:05:39.750 --> 00:05:43.320
No need to configure
Pub/Sub, Scheduler, even Arc.

00:05:43.320 --> 00:05:47.670
We only needed to make a REST
API call to our microservices.

00:05:47.670 --> 00:05:48.600
MARTIN OMANDER: OK.

00:05:48.600 --> 00:05:51.452
Any drawbacks you noticed?

00:05:51.452 --> 00:05:52.410
GUILLAUME LAFORGE: Yes.

00:05:52.410 --> 00:05:56.485
Since it's a different approach,
you got to learn it, right?

00:05:56.485 --> 00:05:57.360
MARTIN OMANDER: Yeah.

00:05:57.360 --> 00:06:00.030
Also, you're actually
transforming some code

00:06:00.030 --> 00:06:02.490
into YAML configuration.

00:06:02.490 --> 00:06:05.617
Does that make it harder
to test and debug?

00:06:05.617 --> 00:06:07.450
GUILLAUME LAFORGE: Well
to some extent, yes.

00:06:07.450 --> 00:06:09.075
But on the other
hand, it's really just

00:06:09.075 --> 00:06:11.038
declarative API calls.

00:06:11.038 --> 00:06:11.830
MARTIN OMANDER: OK.

00:06:11.830 --> 00:06:16.170
And in terms of architecture of
the system, any other impacts?

00:06:16.170 --> 00:06:19.380
GUILLAUME LAFORGE: We lost
some parallelism along the way

00:06:19.380 --> 00:06:23.460
in favor of a more sequential
and predictable chain of calls.

00:06:23.460 --> 00:06:26.783
We may have lost a bit
of flexibility as well.

00:06:26.783 --> 00:06:27.700
MARTIN OMANDER: Right.

00:06:27.700 --> 00:06:29.430
I guess with event
driven systems,

00:06:29.430 --> 00:06:31.170
it's easy to extend the system.

00:06:31.170 --> 00:06:33.310
You just fire up some
new type of event.

00:06:33.310 --> 00:06:33.810
Right?

00:06:33.810 --> 00:06:35.060
GUILLAUME LAFORGE: Yeah, true.

00:06:35.060 --> 00:06:38.460
Or plug into existing even
streams to add new services.

00:06:38.460 --> 00:06:39.690
MARTIN OMANDER: Ah, right.

00:06:39.690 --> 00:06:41.470
But overall, it was
a good experience?

00:06:41.470 --> 00:06:42.720
GUILLAUME LAFORGE: Definitely.

00:06:42.720 --> 00:06:45.870
If you can draw your business
processes in a flowchart,

00:06:45.870 --> 00:06:48.645
be sure to give Workflows a try.

00:06:48.645 --> 00:06:49.520
MARTIN OMANDER: Cool.

00:06:49.520 --> 00:06:51.910
And how can we learn
more about Workflows

00:06:51.910 --> 00:06:53.463
and get our hands on it?

00:06:53.463 --> 00:06:54.880
GUILLAUME LAFORGE:
Mete and I have

00:06:54.880 --> 00:06:57.220
been running a serverless
workshop including

00:06:57.220 --> 00:07:00.820
both event driven and
orchestrated approaches.

00:07:00.820 --> 00:07:03.700
You can freely follow the
collabs at your own pace.

00:07:03.700 --> 00:07:06.430
And the code of
our Pic-a-Daily app

00:07:06.430 --> 00:07:08.110
is all open source on GitHub.

00:07:08.110 --> 00:07:12.300
And you can also read our
article with all the details.

00:07:12.300 --> 00:07:13.300
MARTIN OMANDER: Awesome.

00:07:13.300 --> 00:07:15.760
It's time to read about it
and get our hands on the code.

00:07:15.760 --> 00:07:16.940
Thank you, Guillaume.

00:07:16.940 --> 00:07:19.100
GUILLAUME LAFORGE:
Thank you, Martin.

00:07:19.100 --> 00:07:23.350
[MUSIC PLAYING]

